// draft
// drafted
type Item = { value: Text, raises: Text[] }

filter_except(items: Item[], exceptions: Text[]) -> Result<Item[], Item>
	note "keep the items the validator accepts; an item's raises lists its exception's class and that class's ancestors, empty when the validator returns, and an exception is caught when one of those classes is among the exceptions"
	# The output is Ok exactly when every item on which the validator raises has some class of its exception among the exceptions.

	# Every element of an Ok output is an item on which the validator returns, with an empty raises.

	# An Ok output is drawn from the items.

	# Every item left out of an Ok output is one on which the validator raises an exception with some class among the exceptions.

	# An Ok output keeps the items' order.

	# An Err output carries an input item on which the validator raises an exception with no class among the exceptions.

	# Every item before the Err item in the input either has an empty raises or has some class of its exception among the exceptions.

	// example wanted: the docstring case, values ['1', '2', 'three', '4', None] with 'three' raising ValueError and None raising TypeError, exceptions [ValueError, TypeError]: which items survive
	// example wanted: no items at all: what an empty input gives
	// example wanted: an item raising ZeroDivisionError (ancestors ArithmeticError, Exception) with exceptions [ArithmeticError]: the ancestor decides whether it is caught
	// example wanted: valid items followed by one raising KeyError with exceptions [ValueError]: the items yielded before the raise are not returned
	// example wanted: two items raising uncaught exceptions: the earlier one is the one reported
	// example wanted: an empty exceptions list with one item that raises: nothing is caught
